// Interview problems: Soft Robotics (24-673) interview problems written from the lecture slides. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_BY=window.EXAMPLES_BY||{}).sr=[{"id":"1.1","title":"Projecting a soft robotics market forecast","q":"An analyst report gives a soft robotics market size of 300.5 million USD in 2018 and a projection of 2.32 billion USD in 2024. Assuming constant compound annual growth between the two years, find the annual growth rate, then predict the market size in 2021.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: compound growth V(n) = V0 (1+g)^n.\n2. Endpoint ratio: 2.32e9 / 300.5e6 = 7.72 over n = 6 years.\n3. g = 7.72^(1/6) - 1; ln 7.72 = 2.044, divided by 6 = 0.3406, exp = 1.4057, so g = 0.406.\n4. 2021 is n = 3: V = 300.5 x 1.4057^3 = 300.5 x 2.778 = 835 million USD.\n5. Comment: this matches the quoted 40.6% CAGR; the extrapolation is only as good as the constant-growth assumption.","answers":["Growth rate about 40.6% per year","2021 market about 835 million USD"]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Force and contraction of a braided pneumatic muscle","q":"An ideal McKibben pneumatic muscle has braid fiber length b = 0.30 m, each fiber making n = 3 turns around the tube, and an initial braid angle of 30° to the axis. It is pressurized to a gauge pressure of 300 kPa. Ignoring tube elasticity and friction, find the axial blocked force and the maximum free contraction (fraction of initial length).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: virtual work for an ideal braided muscle (Chou and Hannaford): F = (P b^2 / (4 pi n^2)) (3 cos^2 theta - 1).\n2. Geometry factor: b^2/(4 pi n^2) = 0.09/(4 pi x 9) = 7.958e-4 m^2.\n3. P x factor = 3e5 x 7.958e-4 = 238.7 N.\n4. At theta = 30°: 3cos^2 = 2.25, so (2.25 - 1) = 1.25 and F = 298 N.\n5. Force falls to zero when 3cos^2 theta = 1, i.e. theta = 54.7°.\n6. Muscle length L = b cos theta: L0 = 0.3 x 0.866 = 0.260 m, Lmin = 0.3 x 0.577 = 0.173 m.\n7. Contraction = 1 - 0.577/0.866 = 0.333.","answers":["Blocked force about 298 N","Maximum free contraction about 33%, at braid angle 54.7°"]},{"id":"1.3","title":"Payload of a two-finger soft pinch grasp","q":"A pneumatic soft gripper pinches a rigid object between two opposing fingers. Each finger presses with a normal force of 4.0 N, and the friction coefficient between finger and object is 0.60. Find the maximum object mass that can be held against gravity, and the mass rated if a safety factor of 2 is used (g = 9.81 m/s²).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: static friction on two contact patches balances weight.\n2. Friction capacity: 2 x mu x N = 2 x 0.60 x 4.0 = 4.8 N.\n3. Maximum mass: 4.8 / 9.81 = 0.489 kg.\n4. With safety factor 2: rated weight 2.4 N, mass 0.245 kg.\n5. Note that compliance helps conformity, raising effective contact area, but the rating still depends on the friction coefficient being reliable when wet or dusty.","answers":["Maximum mass about 0.49 kg","Rated mass with safety factor 2 about 0.24 kg"]},{"id":"1.4","title":"Gripper concept for packaged food handling","q":"A food packaging line must pick items such as fruit, bread and bagged snacks of varying shape and fragility at about 1 item per second, with frequent washdown. Propose a soft gripper concept: choose the actuation, material, sensing and control, and justify your choices and trade-offs.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Actuation: pneumatic multi-finger (pneu-net or fin-ray style) because pressure control gives gentle, conformal grasp, light weight and no electronics at the fingertips.\n2. Material: food-safe silicone (e.g., platinum-cure, Shore 00-30 to A-30) that survives washdown; molded, replaceable fingers.\n3. Sensing: pressure or flow feedback plus optionally a simple vision system to pick item pose; add soft capacitive or optical contact sensing if verification is needed.\n4. Speed trade-off: soft fingers are slower than rigid ones because pneumatic volume must be filled; use short tubing, fast valves and a regulated supply.\n5. Trade-offs: compliance tolerates shape variation but limits positional precision and payload; wear and hygiene require easy replacement.","answers":["Pneumatic silicone fingers: conformal, gentle, washable","Pressure and vision feedback; speed limited by air volume","Trade-off between adaptability and precision/payload"]},{"id":"1.5","title":"Deciding whether a device counts as a robot","q":"Two devices are proposed: (a) a millimeter-scale soft sheet that crawls and swims when an external magnet is moved around it, and (b) a stretchable strain sensor patch for the skin. Using the NSF definition of a robot as intelligence embodied in an engineered construct, argue whether each is a robot and what a soft robotics researcher would call them.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Definition: robot needs information processing (intelligence) and a construct able to sense and move or alter its environment.\n2. Device (a): soft-material mechanics drives motion and it moves in its environment, but intelligence and sensing reside in the external operator and magnet; it is a soft actuated machine, a robot in the formal sense only if closed-loop control is added.\n3. Device (b): mechanically passive, no action on the environment; it is a soft sensor or stretchable electronic, not a robot, though it could be a component of one.\n4. Conclusion: soft robotics includes all of these because of soft-material mechanics, so soft matter engineering is the more accurate umbrella.","answers":["(a) soft actuator or machine, robot only with onboard or closed-loop intelligence","(b) not a robot: passive soft sensor or electronic","Field is better named soft matter engineering"]},{"id":"2.1","title":"Tip deflection of a soft rod under small load","q":"A solid silicone rod of diameter 10 mm and length 100 mm (Young\u0027s modulus 1.0 MPa) is clamped at one end, and a transverse tip force of 0.02 N is applied. Using Euler-Bernoulli cantilever theory, estimate the tip deflection and decide whether small-deflection theory is justified.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: cantilever with point load, delta = F L^3 / (3 E I).\n2. Second moment of area: I = pi d^4 / 64 = pi (0.01)^4 / 64 = 4.91e-10 m^4.\n3. Flexural rigidity EI = 1.0e6 x 4.91e-10 = 4.91e-4 N m^2.\n4. delta = 0.02 x (0.1)^3 / (3 x 4.91e-4) = 2e-5 / 1.473e-3 = 0.0136 m.\n5. Ratio delta/L = 0.136, so linear theory is marginal, roughly acceptable; at 5 times the load (about 68 mm, 0.68 L) it would clearly fail and a nonlinear or FEM model is needed.","answers":["Tip deflection about 13.6 mm","delta/L = 0.14: linear theory marginal; invalid at larger loads"]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Bending curvature of a three-chamber micro actuator","q":"A Suzumori-type actuator is a rubber cylinder of 4 mm outer diameter and 20 mm length with three air chambers. Only one chamber is pressurized, to 300 kPa. The chamber cross-section is 1.0 mm², its centroid lies 1.0 mm from the axis, and the rubber modulus is 2.0 MPa. Treating the cross-section as a solid circle for stiffness, estimate the moment, curvature and bending angle.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the pressure force acts at an offset from the axis, giving a bending moment, and linear beam bending gives curvature.\n2. Force: F = P A = 3e5 x 1.0e-6 = 0.30 N.\n3. Moment: M = F d = 0.30 x 1.0e-3 = 3.0e-4 N m.\n4. I = pi d^4/64 = pi (0.004)^4 / 64 = 1.257e-11 m^4, EI = 2e6 x 1.257e-11 = 2.51e-5 N m^2.\n5. Curvature kappa = M/EI = 11.9 1/m, radius 84 mm.\n6. Bending angle = kappa L = 11.9 x 0.02 = 0.239 rad = 13.7°.\n7. Assumptions: small strain, ignoring axial extension and chamber voids that reduce stiffness (true bend is larger).","answers":["Curvature about 12 1/m (radius about 84 mm)","Bending angle about 0.24 rad (14°), an optimistic stiffness estimate"]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Capacitance change of a squeezed soft capacitive sensor","q":"A parallel-plate soft capacitor has an incompressible elastomer dielectric and an initial capacitance of 10.0 pF. Pressing it reduces the dielectric thickness by 10% under uniform compression, and the electrodes stretch with the dielectric. Find the new capacitance and relative change.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: C = epsilon A / d.\n2. Incompressibility: volume A d is constant, so thickness ratio 0.9 means area ratio 1/0.9 = 1.111.\n3. C/C0 = (A/A0) / (d/d0) = 1.111 / 0.9 = 1.235.\n4. C = 12.35 pF.\n5. Relative change: 23.5%, versus 11.1% if the area were held fixed, so the lateral expansion of an incompressible dielectric roughly doubles the sensitivity.","answers":["C about 12.3 pF","Relative change about 23.5%"]},{"id":"2.4","title":"Evaluating a candidate paper for a class review","q":"You are asked to choose and critique a paper about a new soft actuator for a seminar. Describe the criteria you would use to judge whether it is high impact, and how you would structure a critique of merits and limitations.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Selection criteria: novel material or architecture; clear robotics application; theoretical model; experimental validation; thorough characterization; citation count; respectable venue.\n2. Presentation structure: background and motivation, paper overview, theory, experiments, applications, then comments.\n3. Merits: what is new versus prior work, whether model agrees with data, whether design guidelines result.\n4. Limitations: reproducibility of fabrication, clarity of notation, limited test conditions, scale, durability, cycle life, missing controls.\n5. Broader context: compare with existing actuators on strain, force, speed and efficiency.","answers":["Novelty, application, model plus experiment, citations, venue","Critique: merits, reproducibility, limitations, broader comparison"]},{"id":"2.5","title":"Planning a validated soft gripper term project","q":"Plan a one-semester project on a soft pneumatic gripper that must pick a 200 g object. Specify your design, model, experiments and what result would count as validating your hypothesis.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Hypothesis: a given finger geometry and pressure range lifts 200 g with a safety factor.\n2. Design: choose actuation (pneumatic chambers in silicone), materials and finger geometry; fabricate by molding.\n3. Model: beam or nonlinear analytic model for bending vs pressure, FEM for comparison, friction-based pinch capacity.\n4. Experiments: measure bending angle vs pressure, tip force vs pressure, pinch load test with different objects and surfaces.\n5. Validation: model predictions within about 10-20% of measurements and the gripper lifts the target with margin; report errors and failure modes.\n6. Deliverables: prototype, report, and conference-style presentation.","answers":["Design, model, measurement loop with quantified agreement","Success criterion: lifts 200 g with margin and model within about 20%"]},{"id":"3.1","title":"Force and contraction of a braided pneumatic muscle","q":"A McKibben muscle has a thin, ideal elastomer bladder inside an inextensible braid. Each braid fiber has length b = 100 mm and winds n = 2 turns around the tube. At the initial state the braid angle to the axis is 30° and the gauge pressure is 400 kPa. Neglect bladder elasticity and friction. (a) Find the muscle length and radius at 30°. (b) Find the axial force it can exert at 30°. (c) What is the largest possible contraction from the 30° length when pressure is held constant?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: braid kinematics plus virtual work, F = -p dV/dL.\n2. Kinematics: L = b cosθ, R = b sinθ/(2πn), V = πR²L = b³ sin²θ cosθ/(4πn²).\n3. Force: dV/dL = -b²(3cos²θ - 1)/(4πn²), so F = p b²(3cos²θ - 1)/(4πn²).\n4. (a) L = 100 cos30° = 86.6 mm; R = 100 × 0.5/(2π × 2) = 3.98 mm.\n5. (b) b² = 0.01 m², 3cos²30° - 1 = 1.25, 4πn² = 50.27; F = 400e3 × 0.01 × 1.25 / 50.27 = 99.5 N. Check: πR²p = 19.9 N, times (3cos²θ - 1)/sin²θ = 5 gives 99.5 N.\n6. (c) Force vanishes at cosθ = 1/√3 (θ = 54.7°), so L* = b/√3 = 57.7 mm. Contraction = (86.6 - 57.7)/86.6 = 33%. A real muscle stops earlier because bladder elasticity and end effects add load.","answers":["L = 86.6 mm, R = 3.98 mm","F ≈ 99.5 N","Maximum contraction ≈ 33% (L* = 57.7 mm)"]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Voltage limit and strain of a dielectric elastomer film","q":"A dielectric elastomer film has undeformed thickness t0 = 100 µm, shear modulus μ = 100 kPa and permittivity ε = 3.1×10⁻¹¹ F/m. Use the neo-Hookean, equal-biaxial model in which the thickness stretch obeys λ⁶ = 1 - Φ̂² with Φ̂ = (Φ/t0)√(ε/μ). (a) Find the critical voltage Φcr = t0√(μ/ε). (b) At Φ = 3 kV, find the Maxwell stress based on the undeformed thickness. (c) At Φ = 3 kV find λ and the resulting in-plane linear stretch.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the Maxwell stress compresses the film; equilibrium with the neo-Hookean through-thickness stress gives λ(Φ̂).\n2. (a) √(μ/ε) = √(1e5/3.1e-11) = 5.68e7 V/m, so Φcr = 1e-4 m × 5.68e7 V/m = 5.68 kV.\n3. (b) Field E = Φ/t0 = 3000/1e-4 = 3.0e7 V/m. σ = εE² = 3.1e-11 × 9e14 = 2.8e4 Pa = 28 kPa.\n4. (c) Φ̂ = 3000/5680 = 0.528; Φ̂² = 0.279; λ⁶ = 0.721, so λ = 0.721^(1/6) = 0.947 (thickness down 5.3%).\n5. Incompressibility: in-plane stretch = λ^(-1/2) = 1.028, i.e. 2.8% linear strain (about 5.6% area strain).\n6. Conclusion: at about half of Φcr the strain is small; useful strain requires pre-stretch, thinner films or stacking.","answers":["Φcr ≈ 5.7 kV","Maxwell stress ≈ 28 kPa","λ ≈ 0.947; in-plane stretch ≈ 1.028 (2.8% linear strain)"]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Work, stroke and electrical energy of an SMA wire","q":"A NiTi shape memory wire of diameter 0.15 mm and length 100 mm recovers 4% strain while pulling against a constant stress of 200 MPa on heating. Overall electrical-to-mechanical efficiency is 4%, and one full heat-cool cycle takes 2 s. Find (a) force and stroke, (b) mechanical work per cycle and work density, (c) electrical energy per cycle and average electrical power.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: work = force × stroke; electrical input = work / efficiency.\n2. Area A = π(0.075 mm)² = 0.01767 mm² = 1.767e-8 m².\n3. (a) F = σA = 200e6 × 1.767e-8 = 3.53 N. Stroke = 0.04 × 100 mm = 4 mm.\n4. (b) W = F × stroke = 3.53 × 0.004 = 14.1 mJ. Wire volume = 1.767e-8 × 0.1 = 1.767e-9 m³, so work density = σε = 200e6 × 0.04 = 8 MJ/m³ (= 14.1 mJ / 1.767e-9 m³).\n5. (c) E_el = W/0.04 = 0.353 J per cycle. Average power = 0.353/2 = 0.18 W.\n6. Remark: very high work density but low efficiency and slow cycle (0.5 Hz), consistent with SMA being strong but slow and wasteful; most input energy leaves as heat that must be shed to reset.","answers":["F ≈ 3.5 N, stroke 4 mm","W ≈ 14 mJ; work density 8 MJ/m³","Electrical energy ≈ 0.35 J per cycle, average power ≈ 0.18 W"]},{"id":"3.4","title":"Choose actuation for a soft fruit-picking gripper","q":"Design a soft gripper to pick ripe strawberries (about 20-30 g each, easily bruised, wet and sticky surface) from plants in a greenhouse at about 1 pick per 3 s. The arm can supply compressed air or electrical power. Choose the actuation technology and body architecture, the material and stiffness range, and sensing. Justify using stress, strain, speed and safety, and state one failure mode you would test.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: low contact pressure, strain-limited fingers, 3 s cycle (0.3 Hz is easy), wet environment, safe near people.\n2. Actuation: pneumatic fiber-reinforced or pneu-net fingers (silicone of a few MPa, Shore 20-30A, 50-150 kPa operating pressure). Large strain and gentle force come from cheap compressed air; bandwidth is more than adequate; no high voltage near water (rules out DEA/HASEL); SMA is too slow and hot for fruit.\n3. Architecture: 2-3 fingers curling toward a strain-limiting layer, soft pads, compliant wrist; pressure sets grip force (about 2-5 N is enough).\n4. Sensing: pressure in each finger plus embedded liquid-metal or capacitive strain sensors for contact; slip or stem-cut detection.\n5. Test: leakage or burst at the chamber wall after thousands of cycles, and bruise tests versus pressure.","answers":["Pneumatic soft bending fingers (silicone, 50-150 kPa): gentle, large-strain, low-cost, wet-safe","Strain-limiting layer and soft pads keep contact pressure low; pressure and strain sensing for grip control","Rule out DEA (kV, wet) and SMA (slow, hot); test leak/fatigue and bruising"]},{"id":"3.5","title":"Compare actuators against biological muscle for an untethered robot","q":"An untethered 50 g soft crawler needs actuators that match skeletal muscle (about 100-400 kPa stress, 20-40% strain, 1-10 Hz) while carrying only a small battery and no air supply. Compare shape memory alloy, dielectric elastomer / HASEL, pneumatic and liquid crystal elastomer actuators, recommend one, and explain the main trade-offs and one mitigation for its weakness.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Match each to the muscle window: SMA has high stress and moderate strain, but 3-5% efficiency, 0.1-1 Hz, 10-100k cycles, and runs hot. DEA/HASEL: kV drive but fast (ms to s), muscle-like strain, little current; HASEL self-heals. Pneumatic: high stress and strain but needs pump, valves and tether. LCE: very large strain but slow thermal cycle and moderate stress.\n2. The untethered constraint penalizes pneumatics (compressor mass) and power-hungry SMA/LCE heaters (efficiency).\n3. Recommendation: HASEL or stacked DEA with a miniature high-voltage converter; low current makes efficient use of a small battery.\n4. Weaknesses: kilovolt electronics and insulation, breakdown, pull-in limit Φcr = t0√(μ/ε). Mitigate with liquid dielectrics (self-healing), thin layers or stacks to lower voltage, and encapsulation.\n5. Alternative: SMA with antagonistic pairs if forces are high and low speed is acceptable.","answers":["Recommend HASEL or stacked DEA: muscle-like strain and speed with low current for a small battery","SMA is strong but slow and 3-5% efficient; pneumatics need a pump and tether; LCE is slow","Mitigate kV drive and breakdown by liquid dielectric (self-healing), thin stacked films and encapsulation"]},{"id":"4.1","title":"Resistance change of a stretched liquid-metal strain sensor","q":"A strain sensor is a straight microchannel of length 60 mm with a rectangular cross-section 200 µm wide and 100 µm high, filled with EGaIn of conductivity 3.4×10⁶ S/m, embedded in a soft elastomer. The channel is stretched uniaxially by 40% along its length. Assume the liquid metal keeps constant volume, fills the channel completely, and contact resistance is negligible. Find (a) the initial resistance, (b) the resistance at 40% strain and the change, (c) the secant gauge factor over this range.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: R = ρL/(wh) with ρ = 1/σ; constant volume means length stretch λ implies cross-section area shrinks by λ.\n2. ρ = 1/3.4e6 = 2.94e-7 Ω·m. Area = 200e-6 × 100e-6 = 2.0e-8 m².\n3. (a) R0 = 2.94e-7 × 0.06 / 2.0e-8 = 0.882 Ω.\n4. (b) λ = 1.4: length × 1.4, area / 1.4, so R = R0 λ² = 0.882 × 1.96 = 1.73 Ω. ΔR = 0.85 Ω (+96%).\n5. (c) Secant gauge factor = (ΔR/R0)/ε = 0.96/0.40 = 2.4 (it approaches 2 at small strain, since R/R0 = (1+ε)²).\n6. Remark: compare with a metal foil gauge (GF about 2) but with 40% range instead of under 1%; low absolute resistance means a Wheatstone bridge or a precision amplifier is needed.","answers":["R0 ≈ 0.88 Ω","R ≈ 1.73 Ω at 40% strain; ΔR ≈ 0.85 Ω (+96%)","Secant gauge factor ≈ 2.4"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Pressure response of a liquid-metal microchannel sensor","q":"A liquid-metal pressure sensor has a channel of width w = 500 µm and height h = 100 µm in an elastomer with E = 300 kPa and ν = 0.5. Its fractional resistance change under surface pressure p follows ΔR/R0 = 1/[1 − 2(1−ν²) w p/(E h)] − 1. (a) Find ΔR/R0 at p = 5 kPa. (b) Find the pressure at which the resistance has doubled. (c) At what pressure does the model predict the channel closes (resistance diverges)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: define x = 2(1−ν²) w p/(E h) so ΔR/R0 = 1/(1−x) − 1.\n2. Constants: 2(1−ν²) = 1.5; w/h = 5; E = 3.0e5 Pa, so x = 1.5 × 5 × p / 3.0e5 = p/40000 Pa.\n3. (a) p = 5000 Pa: x = 0.125; ΔR/R0 = 1/0.875 − 1 = 0.143, i.e. 14%.\n4. (b) Doubling means ΔR/R0 = 1, so 1/(1−x) = 2, x = 0.5, p = 20 kPa.\n5. (c) Divergence at x = 1, p = 40 kPa.\n6. Comment: sensitivity is nonlinear, flat at low pressure and steep near closure; a stiffer elastomer or smaller w/h shifts the range upward.","answers":["ΔR/R0 ≈ 14% at 5 kPa","Resistance doubles at p = 20 kPa","Channel closes (R diverges) at p = 40 kPa"]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Capacitance of a stretched ionic skin sensor","q":"A stretchable capacitive skin has a dielectric elastomer layer 100 µm thick and 20 mm × 20 mm in area, relative permittivity 3.0 (ε0 = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m), between two ionic hydrogel electrodes. Treat it as a parallel-plate capacitor, ignore edge and double-layer effects and treat the dielectric as incompressible. (a) Find the unstretched capacitance. (b) The skin is stretched uniaxially by a stretch ratio of 1.5 with free lateral contraction; find the new capacitance. (c) The skin is instead pressed so the thickness falls by 20% with no lateral constraint; find the new capacitance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: C = εA/d with volume conserved, so A d = const.\n2. ε = 3.0 × 8.85e-12 = 2.655e-11 F/m. A0 = 4.0e-4 m².\n3. (a) C0 = 2.655e-11 × 4.0e-4 / 1.0e-4 = 1.06e-10 F = 106 pF.\n4. (b) Uniaxial incompressible: area stretch λ^(1/2) = 1.2247 and thickness λ^(-1/2) = 0.8165, so C = C0 × (1.2247/0.8165) = C0 λ = 1.5 × 106 = 159 pF (+53 pF).\n5. (c) d = 0.8 d0, area = A0/0.8 = 1.25 A0, so C = C0 × 1.25/0.8 = 1.5625 × 106 = 166 pF.\n6. Remark: the sensor is linear in stretch (gauge factor 1) and also responds to pressure, so strain and pressure signals are coupled and must be separated by layout or multiple electrodes.","answers":["C0 ≈ 106 pF","C ≈ 159 pF at 1.5 uniaxial stretch (C proportional to λ)","C ≈ 166 pF after 20% thickness compression"]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Design a sensing skin for a soft robotic hand","q":"Design a stretchable tactile and joint-angle sensing skin for the fingers of a soft pneumatic robot hand. Fingers bend to about 90° (surface strains up to 30%), cycle thousands of times, and must resolve touches of a few kPa. Choose conductor and sensing technology, geometry, wiring and read-out, and justify against deterministic wavy/serpentine metal, filled elastomers, hydrogels and liquid metal. Name main failure modes.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: 30% strain, low stiffness (comparable to the elastomer, kPa-MPa), thousands of cycles, pressure sensitivity of a few kPa, electrical connection to a microcontroller.\n2. Options: wavy/serpentine metal works but is limited by strain concentration and needs lithography; Ag- or carbon-filled elastomers are cheap but drift and show hysteresis at 30% strain; hydrogels are transparent but dry out; liquid metal gives high conductivity with no fatigue.\n3. Recommended: EGaIn microchannels in silicone, capacitive or resistive; serpentine channels along the finger for strain (R ∝ λ²), a layer of wider channels near the surface for pressure (ΔR from channel closing).\n4. Wiring: LM traces to a flexible board with direct IC (pins embedded in LM) and a bridge or capacitance-to-digital converter; temperature compensation with a reference channel.\n5. Failure modes: oxide/leak at inlets, delamination between layers, cross-talk of strain and pressure signals, hysteresis of the elastomer.","answers":["Liquid-metal (EGaIn) channels in silicone: serpentine strain channels and a pressure layer, with R ∝ λ² read-out","Chosen for high conductivity at large strain and fatigue life; filled elastomers drift, hydrogels dry, thin metal films crack","Watch inlet leakage, delamination and strain/pressure cross-talk; compensate for temperature"]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Wearable circuit that tolerates stretch, cuts and ICs","q":"A designer must build a patch that adheres to the forearm, contains a microcontroller and several sensors, survives 50% stretch and stays functional after being punctured by sharp objects. Compare a noncoplanar mesh/serpentine silicon design with a liquid-metal elastomer circuit, recommend one architecture, and explain how rigid chips are connected, how strain is distributed, and what the main trade-offs are.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Mesh/serpentine: high-performance silicon islands are rigid and bridges (nm-thick Au) buckle or unfold; strain is shared by bridges; stretchability depends on pre-strain and geometry (70% reported); manufacturing is lithographic and costly; punctures sever bridges (no repair).\n2. Liquid metal: EGaIn traces are intrinsically stretchable (above 50%), self-heal in the droplet-composite version (pathways reform around damage), printed or laser patterned cheaply; resistance changes as R ∝ λ², so strain sensitivity must be small or compensated; the oxide skin requires encapsulation.\n3. Chips: embed IC pins directly in liquid-metal traces (no solder); the rigid chip carries no strain, so stiff-to-soft transition regions use gradual stiffness or island encapsulation.\n4. Recommendation: LM-composite circuit with a self-healing layer and rigid-chip islands, if cuts or punctures are expected.\n5. Trade-offs: lower conductivity and higher resistance than copper, slower manufacturing for fine pitch, leakage risk at large damage.","answers":["Liquid-metal droplet composite with embedded IC pins; self-heals and stretches above 50%","Strain is carried by the soft elastomer; rigid chips sit on islands with graded stiffness transitions","Mesh/serpentine silicon gives better electronics but is costly and cannot recover from puncture"]},{"id":"5.1","title":"Force to stretch a silicone strip threefold","q":"A silicone strip of rectangular cross-section 5 mm × 2 mm (reference area A0) has Young\u0027s modulus E = 0.8 MPa at small strain and behaves as an incompressible neo-Hookean solid. It is pulled uniaxially to a stretch ratio λ = 3. Find the force F, the nominal (engineering) stress, and the true (Cauchy) stress. Also state what Hooke\u0027s law F = EA0(λ − 1) would predict.","figs":["figures/sr/fig5-15.webp"],"figDesc":"Cylinder/bar in reference configuration (length L0, area A0) and deformed configuration (length L = L0 + ΔL, area A) loaded by an axial force F at both ends.","sol":"1. Approach: neo-Hookean uniaxial law with coefficient matched to Hooke\u0027s law at small strain.\n2. C1 = E/6 = 0.8/6 = 0.1333 MPa.\n3. Force: F = 2 C1 A0 (λ − λ^-2) = (E A0/3)(λ − λ^-2).\n4. A0 = 5 × 2 = 10 mm². E A0 = 8 N, so F = (8/3)(3 − 1/9) = 2.667 × 2.889 = 7.70 N.\n5. Nominal stress σe = F/A0 = 7.70 N / 10 mm² = 0.770 MPa.\n6. True stress σ = λ σe (incompressible, A = A0/λ) = 3 × 0.770 = 2.31 MPa.\n7. Hooke\u0027s law: F = 8 × (3 − 1) = 16 N, more than twice the neo-Hookean value, because the neo-Hookean curve softens relative to the linear tangent at this stretch.","answers":["F ≈ 7.7 N","Nominal stress ≈ 0.77 MPa; true stress ≈ 2.31 MPa","Hooke\u0027s law gives 16 N (overestimates by about 2×)"]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Voltage limit of a thin dielectric elastomer film","q":"A dielectric elastomer actuator is a soft parallel-plate capacitor with a film of initial thickness t0 = 50 μm, shear modulus μ = 40 kPa and relative permittivity 3.5 (ε0 = 8.854×10⁻¹² F/m). Model the film as neo-Hookean with a thickness stretch λ satisfying λ = (1 − Φ̂²)^(1/6), where Φ̂ = (Φ/t0)·sqrt(ε/μ) and Φ is the applied voltage. Find the critical voltage Φcr, and for Φ = 1000 V the thickness stretch λ and the film thickness.","figs":["figures/sr/fig5-37.webp"],"figDesc":"Photographs of a dielectric elastomer actuator lifting a weight, and a schematic of a stack: conductive elastomer electrode, insulating elastomer dielectric, conductive elastomer electrode, with charges +q and −q on the electrodes and the Maxwell stress acting to squeeze the film.","sol":"1. Approach: use the closed-form equilibrium from minimizing electrical-enthalpy potential energy.\n2. Permittivity: ε = 3.5 × 8.854e-12 = 3.099e-11 F/m.\n3. Critical voltage (Φ̂ = 1): Φcr = t0·sqrt(μ/ε) = 50e-6 × sqrt(4e4 / 3.099e-11) = 50e-6 × sqrt(1.291e15) = 50e-6 × 3.593e7 ≈ 1.80 kV.\n4. At Φ = 1000 V: Φ̂ = 1000/1796 = 0.557; Φ̂² = 0.310.\n5. λ = (1 − 0.310)^(1/6) = 0.690^(0.1667) = exp(ln 0.690 / 6) = exp(−0.0618) = 0.940.\n6. Thickness t = λ t0 = 0.940 × 50 μm ≈ 47.0 μm (6% thinning).\n7. Beyond Φcr no equilibrium exists: electromechanical instability (thinning raises the field, runaway collapse).","answers":["Φcr ≈ 1.8 kV","λ ≈ 0.94 at 1000 V","Thickness ≈ 47 μm"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"McKibben muscle geometry and blocked force","q":"A McKibben pneumatic actuator is idealized as an inextensible helical-fiber cylinder with no wall elasticity. Each fiber has length b = 100 mm and makes n = 2 full turns. At gauge pressure p = 300 kPa the fiber angle to the axis is θ = 30°. Using L = b cosθ and D = b sinθ/(nπ), find the length L and diameter D, and, from the virtual-work relation F = −p dV/dL for the enclosed volume V = πD²L/4, find the blocked tensile force.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: kinematics from helix geometry, then energy balance Π = −pV − FL, dΠ/dL = 0 so F = −p dV/dL.\n2. L = b cosθ = 100 × 0.866 = 86.6 mm.\n3. D = b sinθ/(nπ) = 100 × 0.5/(2π) = 7.96 mm.\n4. Express V in L: R² = b²(1 − L²/b²)/(4π²n²), so V = πR²L = (b²L − L³)/(4π n²).\n5. dV/dL = (b² − 3L²)/(4π n²), so F = p (3L² − b²)/(4π n²) = p b² (3cos²θ − 1)/(4π n²).\n6. Numbers: 3cos²30° − 1 = 1.25; b² = 10⁴ mm²; 4π n² = 50.27. F = 0.3 N/mm² × 10⁴ × 1.25 / 50.27 = 74.6 N.\n7. Check: F → 0 at 3cos²θ = 1, θ = 54.7° (maximum-volume angle), and F is a contraction force for θ \u003c 54.7°.","answers":["L ≈ 86.6 mm","D ≈ 7.96 mm","Blocked force ≈ 75 N"]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Why a linear modulus fails for a stretched elastomer","q":"A colleague characterizes a silicone elastomer (E = 1 MPa from the initial slope) and uses F = EA0(λ − 1) to size an actuator that stretches the elastomer to λ = 4. A materials test shows a neo-Hookean fit works well for this rubber. Explain what physical assumption of Hooke\u0027s law is violated, estimate the percentage error in force at λ = 4, and propose a better modeling approach for the design.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Hooke\u0027s law assumes small strain (about 0.1-1%), linear stress-strain and negligible area change in the load path; elastomers are incompressible with A = A0/λ and deform by entropic chain straightening, so response is nonlinear.\n2. Neo-Hookean force: F = (E A0/3)(λ − λ^-2) with C1 = E/6 matched at λ = 1.\n3. At λ = 4: neo-Hookean F/(E A0) = (4 − 0.0625)/3 = 1.31; Hooke F/(E A0) = 3.\n4. Ratio = 1.31/3 = 0.44, so Hooke\u0027s law overpredicts by about 2.3× (about +129%, or the neo-Hookean value is 56% lower).\n5. Recommendation: use a hyperelastic model (neo-Hookean, or Ogden with μ1 + 2μ2 = E/3 to capture strain stiffening), identify coefficients from uniaxial and biaxial tests, and size the actuator with the energy method (dΠ/dλ = 0).","answers":["Hooke\u0027s law assumes small strain and linearity, not valid at λ = 4","Neo-Hookean force is about 0.44 of the Hooke prediction (about 130% overprediction)","Use hyperelastic (neo-Hookean/Ogden) models with the potential-energy method"]},{"id":"5.5","title":"Soft gripper for fragile produce: actuation and modeling","q":"Design a soft gripper to pick ripe strawberries (soft, bruise at roughly 20 kPa contact pressure) from a conveyor at 1 pick per second. Choose the actuation technology, the elastomer, the fabrication route and a sensing approach, and explain how you would model the finger to set pressure and size it. State the main trade-offs versus a dielectric elastomer actuator solution.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Actuation: pneumatic (pneu-net or fiber-reinforced finger) - high force at low voltage, compliant, fast enough for 1 Hz; DEAs need kV drive and have low force.\n2. Material: low-modulus silicone (Shore 00-30 to A-10, E of 0.05-0.5 MPa, e.g. Ecoflex-type) giving large strain without tearing; tough enough for \u003e10^5 cycles.\n3. Fabrication: cast in 3D-printed molds with platinum-cure RTV-2 silicone, embed an inextensible layer to bend, bond the halves.\n4. Sensing: embedded EGaIn strain channel or capacitive pad for contact/curvature; pressure feedback to limit contact below 20 kPa.\n5. Modeling: finite elasticity (neo-Hookean/Ogden), energy method with p as input, or FEA; contact force ≈ pressure × area limits chosen p.\n6. Trade-offs: pneumatics need tubing and a pump or valve and have hysteresis; DEAs are silent and lightweight but limited by Φcr and breakdown.","answers":["Pneumatic soft finger in low-modulus silicone with an inextensible layer; cast molding","Embedded liquid-metal or capacitive sensing to keep contact below about 20 kPa","Model with hyperelastic finite elasticity plus energy method; pneumatics give force and low voltage while DEAs need kV and give low force"]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Capacitance of a stretched VHB strain sensor","q":"A soft capacitive strain sensor is a laminate of a 3M VHB dielectric film (thickness t = 0.5 mm, relative permittivity 4.7, ε0 = 8.854×10⁻¹² F/m) between two compliant carbon electrodes of area 20 mm × 10 mm. Find its unstretched capacitance C0. The sensor is then stretched uniaxially along its 20 mm length to a stretch ratio λ = 1.5 (incompressible dielectric, electrodes follow the film). Find the new capacitance and the relative change ΔC/C0.","figs":["figures/sr/fig6-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Exploded schematic of a soft parallel-plate capacitor: a conductive elastomer layer on top, an insulating elastomer (dielectric) in the middle, and a conductive elastomer layer at the bottom.","sol":"1. Approach: parallel-plate formula C = εA/t, then track area and thickness under incompressible uniaxial stretch.\n2. ε = 4.7 × 8.854e-12 = 4.161e-11 F/m.\n3. A = 20 × 10 mm² = 2.0e-4 m²; t = 0.5e-3 m.\n4. C0 = ε A / t = 4.161e-11 × 2.0e-4 / 5.0e-4 = 1.66e-11 F ≈ 16.6 pF.\n5. Under uniaxial stretch with incompressibility, length scales by λ while width and thickness each scale by λ^-1/2. So area A = λ × λ^-1/2 × A0 = λ^(1/2) A0 and thickness t = λ^(-1/2) t0.\n6. C/C0 = (A/A0)/(t/t0) = λ^{1/2}/λ^{-1/2} = λ.\n7. C = 1.5 × 16.6 = 24.9 pF, so ΔC/C0 = +50%: the sensor has gauge factor 1, linear in strain, matching C = λC0.","answers":["C0 ≈ 16.6 pF","C at λ = 1.5 ≈ 24.9 pF","ΔC/C0 = +50% (C = λC0)"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Resistance of a stretched liquid-metal trace","q":"A microchannel molded in Ecoflex 0030 is filled with EGaIn (conductivity 3.4×10⁶ S/m). The channel is 50 mm long with a rectangular cross-section of 0.5 mm × 0.1 mm. Find its resistance. If the elastomer is stretched uniformly along the channel by λ = 1.5, with the liquid metal incompressible and filling the channel, find the new resistance.","figs":["figures/sr/fig6-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Fabrication photos: a 3D-printed cross-shaped mold with a penny for scale, the mold filled with cast Ecoflex 0030, and the cured elastomer with channels being injected with gallium-indium.","sol":"1. Approach: R = L/(σA); under stretch, volume V = AL of the liquid metal is conserved.\n2. A = 0.5 mm × 0.1 mm = 5.0e-8 m²; L = 0.050 m.\n3. R0 = L/(σA) = 0.050 / (3.4e6 × 5.0e-8) = 0.050/0.170 = 0.294 Ω.\n4. Stretch: L = λL0 and, by incompressibility, A = A0/λ.\n5. R = (λL0)/(σ A0/λ) = λ² R0.\n6. R = 2.25 × 0.294 = 0.662 Ω (a 125% increase for a 50% strain).\n7. Compare a solid metal film which would crack; here the resistance stays low and varies smoothly, so the trace can also be used as a strain sensor.","answers":["R0 ≈ 0.29 Ω","R at λ = 1.5 ≈ 0.66 Ω (R = λ²R0)"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Casting schedule for a two-part silicone at elevated temperature","q":"A two-part platinum-cure silicone (Ecoflex-like) has a pot life of 30 minutes and a demold time of 4 hours at 23 °C. Using the rule of thumb that reaction times halve for each additional 10 °C, find the pot life and demold time if the mold is cured at 53 °C. A mixing, degassing and pouring cycle takes 12 minutes: is it feasible at 53 °C if the mix is also kept at 53 °C?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: time scales as 2^(−ΔT/10 °C).\n2. ΔT = 53 − 23 = 30 °C, so three halvings: factor 2³ = 8.\n3. Pot life = 30 min / 8 = 3.75 min.\n4. Demold time = 4 h / 8 = 0.5 h = 30 min.\n5. Feasibility: the 12 min cycle exceeds the 3.75 min pot life, so the silicone would gel before the pour is finished and it is not feasible if the mix is at 53 °C.\n6. Fix: mix and degas at room temperature (30 min pot life covers 12 min), pour, then place the filled mold in the oven so only the cure is accelerated; demold in about 30 min instead of 4 h.","answers":["Pot life ≈ 3.75 min at 53 °C","Demold ≈ 30 min","12 min cycle is not feasible hot; mix at room temperature, then heat to cure"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Choosing an elastomer for a pneu-net bending finger","q":"You must pick a silicone for a pneumatic bending finger that must survive 200% strain in its chamber walls, be cast in a 3D-printed mold, and bond to a strain-limiting layer. Candidates: Sylgard-184 (E ≈ 3.9 MPa, 100% elongation, tear 3.4 N/mm), Ecoflex 00-10 (E ≈ 0.1 MPa, 697%, tear 3.9 N/mm, no plasma bond), Dragon Skin 10 slow (E ≈ 0.5 MPa, 507%, tear 22 N/mm, no plasma bond). Which would you pick, what processing properties would you check, and how would you bond the layers?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Screen by elongation: Sylgard-184 fails at 100% elongation, so it is out for 200% wall strain (and is stiff).\n2. Ecoflex vs Dragon Skin: both exceed 200%; Dragon Skin has much higher tear strength (22 vs 3.9 N/mm), favoring durable thin walls, but E = 0.5 MPa needs higher pressure; Ecoflex 00-10 inflates at lower pressure but tears more easily under cyclic loading.\n3. Pick Dragon Skin 10 (or a lower-modulus blend) for durability, or Ecoflex if low pressure is paramount.\n4. Check mixed viscosity (flow into fine mold features, degassing), pot life and demold time (Ecoflex 18-45 min, 3-4 h), shrinkage (\u003c 0.1%).\n5. Bonding: neither bonds by plasma, so cast the base layer and bond with a thin uncured layer of the same silicone (platinum cure) so it co-cures, rather than relying on plasma treatment.","answers":["Reject Sylgard-184 (100% elongation, stiff)","Choose Dragon Skin (tear 22 N/mm) or Ecoflex if low pressure is key","Bond with a thin uncured silicone layer since plasma bonding is unavailable"]},{"id":"6.5","title":"Stretchable sensing glove: materials and fabrication plan","q":"Plan a stretchable sensing glove that tracks finger joint angles for rehabilitation, must tolerate 50% strain on the skin for thousands of cycles and be made at low cost in small batches. Choose the sensing principle, the conductor material, the elastomer, the patterning method and encapsulation, and discuss trade-offs of your choice against an alternative.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Sensing: capacitive (dielectric elastomer) sensors on each joint, since C = λC0 is nearly linear and insensitive to routing; alternative is liquid-metal resistive strain, with R ∝ λ².\n2. Materials: VHB or silicone dielectric with carbon-based (eCAP/cPDMS) electrodes for capacitive; EGaIn in Ecoflex microchannels for resistive.\n3. Fabrication: laser-cut stick-cut-remove lamination (CO2 laser, 10.6 μm), which produced 144 sensors in 45 minutes with about 1.3% capacitance spread; or mold-and-inject for EGaIn.\n4. Encapsulation: cap layers (Tegaderm or silicone) to protect electrodes; serpentine leads and a flexible PCB reader (StretchSense style).\n5. Trade-offs: capacitive sensors have small absolute capacitance (picofarads) needing shielding; liquid metal is highly stretchable and low resistance but needs sealing against leaks.","answers":["Capacitive laminated sensors (C = λC0) with carbon electrodes, laser-cut in batches","Alternative: EGaIn microchannel strain sensor, higher conductivity but leakage risk","Encapsulate and use serpentine leads; note pF-level signal needs shielding"]},{"id":"7.1","title":"Pulse-heating a Nitinol wire","q":"A straight Nitinol (NiTi) actuator wire has diameter 0.15 mm and length 100 mm. Resistivity 0.8×10⁻⁶ Ω·m, density 6450 kg/m³, specific heat 320 J/(kg·K). A fixed 5 V supply is switched across the wire by a transistor for 50 ms. Neglect heat loss during the pulse. Find the wire resistance, current, electrical power, and the temperature rise. If the wire starts at 25 °C and its austenite-finish temperature is 90 °C, does one pulse complete the transformation?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Joule heating into a lumped thermal mass, adiabatic.\n2. Area A = π(0.075 mm)² = 1.767×10⁻⁸ m².\n3. Resistance R = ρL/A = 0.8×10⁻⁶ × 0.1 / 1.767×10⁻⁸ = 4.53 Ω.\n4. Current I = V/R = 5/4.53 = 1.10 A; power P = V²/R = 25/4.53 = 5.5 W.\n5. Energy Q = PΔt = 5.52 × 0.05 = 0.276 J.\n6. Mass m = ηAL = 6450 × 1.767×10⁻⁸ × 0.1 = 1.14×10⁻⁵ kg.\n7. ΔT = Q/(mc) = 0.276 / (1.14×10⁻⁵ × 320) = 76 K (same as V²Δt/(ηρcL²)).\n8. Final temperature about 25 + 76 = 101 °C \u003e 90 °C, so the wire passes A_f in one pulse, ignoring convection and the latent heat of transformation (which would reduce the rise somewhat), so the margin is modest. Shorter wires heat faster since ΔT scales as 1/L².","answers":["R ≈ 4.5 Ω, I ≈ 1.1 A, P ≈ 5.5 W","ΔT ≈ 76 K, final ≈ 101 °C (above A_f = 90 °C, ideal adiabatic case)"]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Stiffness swing of a heated cantilever layer","q":"A thermally tunable cantilever strip of width 10 mm, thickness 1.0 mm and free length 40 mm is clamped at one end. Its modulus is 0.86 GPa when cold and 95 kPa when heated above its transition. Treat it as a homogeneous beam with a point load at the tip. Compute the tip stiffness in each state, the ratio between them, and the tip force needed to deflect it by 5 mm in each state.","figs":["figures/sr/fig7-28.webp"],"figDesc":"Photograph of a laminated strip (galinstan heater, SMP or Field\u0027s metal layer between VHB tape) and a modulus scale marking E_on = 95 kPa and E_off = 0.86 GPa; a tensile test rig at right.","sol":"1. Approach: Euler-Bernoulli cantilever, k = 3EI/L³.\n2. Second moment I = bh³/12 = 0.010 × (0.001)³/12 = 8.33×10⁻¹³ m⁴.\n3. Cold: k_off = 3 × 0.86×10⁹ × 8.33×10⁻¹³ / (0.04)³ = 2.15×10⁻³ / 6.4×10⁻⁵ = 33.6 N/m.\n4. Hot: k_on = k_off × (95×10³ / 0.86×10⁹) = 33.6 × 1.105×10⁻⁴ = 3.7×10⁻³ N/m.\n5. Ratio = E_off/E_on = 9.05×10³ (about 4 orders of magnitude).\n6. Force for δ = 5 mm: F = kδ: cold 0.168 N; hot 1.86×10⁻⁵ N (about 19 µN).\n7. Comment: the hot state is nearly a limp film, so gravity and VHB backing would dominate in practice; the rest of the laminate sets a floor.","answers":["k_off ≈ 34 N/m, k_on ≈ 3.7×10⁻³ N/m","Ratio ≈ 9×10³","Force for 5 mm: ≈ 0.17 N (cold), ≈ 19 µN (hot)"]},{"id":"7.3","title":"McKibben muscle force and contraction","q":"An idealized McKibben muscle has an inextensible braid with initial braid angle θ₀ = 30° (measured from the axis) and initial diameter 10 mm at gauge pressure 300 kPa. Using the standard virtual-work model (force F = (πD²p/4)(3cos²θ − 1)/sin²θ, length L = b·cosθ for braid thread length b), find the blocked (isometric) force at θ₀ and the maximum free contraction ratio if the braid can approach the zero-force angle.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: ideal braid, neglect wall elasticity and end effects.\n2. Cross-sectional area πD²/4 = π(0.01)²/4 = 7.854×10⁻⁵ m².\n3. Pressure force = 7.854×10⁻⁵ × 3×10⁵ = 23.6 N.\n4. Braid factor at 30°: cos²θ = 0.75, so 3cos²θ − 1 = 1.25; sin²θ = 0.25; factor = 5.\n5. Blocked force F = 23.6 × 5 = 118 N.\n6. Zero force when 3cos²θ = 1: θ* = 54.7° (cosθ* = 1/√3 = 0.5774).\n7. Contraction: L/L₀ = cosθ*/cosθ₀ = 0.5774/0.8660 = 0.667, so 33% maximum contraction.\n8. Force falls monotonically from 118 N at zero contraction to 0 at 33%, which is why stiffness rises with pressure.","answers":["Blocked force ≈ 118 N","Zero-force angle 54.7°, maximum contraction ≈ 33%"]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Wearable splint that stiffens on demand","q":"Design a soft wrist splint that is comfortable and compliant during normal wear (modulus below about 1 MPa) but can be locked rigid (above about 100 MPa effective bending stiffness) within 2 s when the wearer needs support. Power is a small battery, and no tethered pump is allowed. Choose the stiffening mechanism, materials, heating or actuation scheme and sensing, and justify against alternatives.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: soft-to-rigid ratio about 100×, response about 2 s, battery power, portable, wearable at skin temperature.\n2. Candidate mechanisms: (a) vacuum layer jamming: fast and robust, but needs a pump and airtight envelope; (b) MR/ER: fast but needs about 1 T / kV/mm to solidify, weak and heavy; (c) thermal: SMP or low-melting alloy with stretchable heater, ratio 10³ to 10⁴, response 10 to 60 s and power of watts.\n3. Pick: layer jamming with a miniature pump or a hybrid: low-melting alloy (Field\u0027s metal) or SMP laminate with a liquid-metal (EGaIn) stretchable heater for large ratio, pre-heated to improve time.\n4. Safety: transition temperature must be above skin tolerance (touch below about 45 °C) while still near body-safe; insulate the layer.\n5. Sensing: strain or IMU for lock command; temperature sensor for the heater control loop.\n6. Trade-offs: thermal is silent and slim but slow; jamming is fast but needs hardware.","answers":["Vacuum layer jamming (fast, robust) or SMP/LMPA laminate with a stretchable liquid-metal heater (largest modulus swing, slower)","Reject MR/ER (needs about 1 T or 1 kV/mm to solidify) and tethered pneumatics","Justify with response time (2 s), power budget, skin-safe temperature and ratio of at least 100×"]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Choosing a muscle for an untethered swimmer","q":"A 20 cm soft swimming robot must carry its own battery and flex its fins at about 1 Hz underwater. Compare shape memory alloy coils, shape memory polymer, and liquid crystal elastomer composites as the actuator. Which would you choose and why? Discuss strain, force, heating and cooling, power, and how to embed heaters and electrical traces.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Needs: roughly 1 Hz cycle, so both heating and cooling must be fast; underwater provides strong convective cooling but also drains heat (power cost); onboard battery limits current.\n2. SMA coils: strong (hundreds of MPa), strain a few percent per wire (more with a coil), Joule heating is fast (ΔT = V²Δt/(ηρcL²)), cooling in water is fast; but power draw is high (several W per wire) and wires are stiff and heavy (6 to 8 g/cm³).\n3. SMP: stiffness change but one-way shape recovery (about 2 min), cooling slow, so unsuitable for 1 Hz cycling.\n4. LCE: reversible contraction on nematic-isotropic transition, low modulus (kPa to MPa), light, large strain; blocked force low, needs heat. An LCE with EGaIn inclusions or channels adds thermal conductivity (about 0.25 to 1.7 W/(m K)) and stretchable heating traces that survive strain.\n5. Choice: SMA coils for force and bandwidth underwater (as in antagonistic SMA soft arms and swimmers); LM-LCE if softness, low weight and conformable heaters outweigh the lower force.","answers":["SMA coils: strongest and fastest cycle in water, but high power and rigid and heavy","SMP unsuitable (slow, one-way recovery); LCE reversible but weak, helped by liquid-metal heaters","Cooling rate sets the cycle frequency: choose the actuator whose heat-up and cool-down fit 1 Hz"]},{"id":"8.1","title":"Limit pressure of an inflated elastomer tube","q":"A long, thin-walled neo-Hookean (Gent with J_m → ∞) elastomer tube has initial radius R₀ = 3 mm, wall thickness t₀ = 0.3 mm, and Young\u0027s modulus E = 1.5 MPa; the material is incompressible and end caps are neglected. With the non-dimensional pressure p̂ = pR₀/(E t₀) and enclosed-volume ratio v = λ₁λ₂², the equilibrium relation is p̂(v) = (1/(3v))·(2v⁴/(1+v²))^{1/3}·(1 − 1/v²), with λ₁ = ((1+v²)/(2v))^{1/3} and λ₂ = (2v⁴/(1+v²))^{1/6}. Find the maximum pressure of the tube and, at that point, the radius, the length ratio L/L₀ and the wall thickness. What does the maximum mean for inflation?","figs":["figures/sr/fig8-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Schematic of a thin-walled tube of initial radius R₀, length L₀, thickness t₀ (left) and the inflated tube of radius R, length L under internal pressure p (right).","sol":"1. Approach: find the maximum of p̂(v) and evaluate the stretches there.\n2. Evaluate p̂ numerically: v = 2 gives 0.237, v = 2.5 gives 0.2475, v = 2.8 gives 0.2498, v = 3.0 gives 0.2499, v = 3.2 gives 0.2493, v = 3.5 gives 0.2475, so the maximum is p̂ ≈ 0.25 at v ≈ 3.\n3. Pressure: p_max = p̂·E t₀/R₀ = 0.25 × 1.5×10⁶ × 0.3/3 = 3.75×10⁴ Pa = 37.5 kPa.\n4. Stretches at v = 3: λ₁ = (10/6)^{1/3} = 1.186; λ₂ = (2·81/10)^{1/6} = 16.2^{1/6} = 1.591 (check λ₁λ₂² = 1.186 × 2.531 = 3.0).\n5. Radius R = λ₂R₀ = 4.77 mm; length ratio L/L₀ = 1.19.\n6. Thickness from incompressibility: t = t₀/(λ₁λ₂) = 0.3/1.887 = 0.159 mm.\n7. Meaning: the p-v curve has a limit point, so under pressure control the tube inflates unstably beyond about 37.5 kPa and a localized bulge forms; strain stiffening (finite J_m) or fiber reinforcement is needed to restabilize.","answers":["p_max ≈ 0.25 E t₀/R₀ ≈ 37.5 kPa at v ≈ 3","R ≈ 4.8 mm (λ₂ ≈ 1.59), L/L₀ ≈ 1.19, t ≈ 0.16 mm","Limit-point instability: bulge or aneurysm-like localization beyond the peak under pressure control"]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Wall stress in an angioplasty balloon","q":"A thin-walled angioplasty balloon has inflated diameter 3.0 mm and wall thickness 20 µm, and is treated as a closed thin cylinder. It is inflated to its nominal pressure of 10 atm (1 atm = 101.3 kPa), and its rated burst pressure is 14 atm. Find the hoop and axial stresses at nominal pressure, the hoop stress at rated pressure, and the minimum material strength needed if a safety factor of 1.5 is required at rated pressure. Comment on whether a latex-type elastomer is appropriate.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: thin-wall relations σ_θ = pR/t, σ_z = pR/(2t), with deformed radius R = 1.5 mm and thickness t = 20 µm.\n2. Nominal p = 10 × 101.3 kPa = 1.013 MPa.\n3. Hoop stress σ_θ = 1.013×10⁶ × 1.5×10⁻³ / 20×10⁻⁶ = 76 MPa.\n4. Axial stress = σ_θ/2 = 38 MPa.\n5. Rated p = 14 atm = 1.419 MPa; hoop stress = 1.419×10⁶ × 75 = 106 MPa.\n6. Required strength with SF 1.5: 1.5 × 106 = 160 MPa.\n7. Latex or silicone have strengths of order 5 to 30 MPa and would stretch a lot, so they would balloon unstably; angioplasty balloons use thin, oriented, high-modulus polymers (e.g. PET, nylon), which stay close to a fixed diameter.","answers":["σ_θ ≈ 76 MPa, σ_z ≈ 38 MPa at 10 atm","σ_θ ≈ 106 MPa at 14 atm; required strength ≈ 160 MPa","Soft elastomers unsuitable; use thin high-modulus oriented polymer"]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Holding force of a layer-jamming joint","q":"A layer-jamming segment for a surgical arm consists of 6 stacked polymer strips, each overlapping its neighbors over an area of 20 mm × 50 mm, enclosed in an airtight sleeve evacuated to 80 kPa below atmosphere (gauge −80 kPa). The friction coefficient between layers is 0.3. Estimate the maximum axial shear force the stack can transmit before slipping, and compare with the unjammed case. If one wanted 300 N, how many layers are needed at the same vacuum and overlap?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: vacuum presses layers together with normal pressure Δp over each interface; Coulomb friction F = μ N.\n2. Interface area A = 0.020 × 0.050 = 1.0×10⁻³ m².\n3. Normal force per interface N = Δp A = 80×10³ × 1.0×10⁻³ = 80 N.\n4. Friction capacity per interface = 0.3 × 80 = 24 N.\n5. Number of interfaces = 6 − 1 = 5, so total shear capacity ≈ 5 × 24 = 120 N (assuming all interfaces share the load equally).\n6. Unjammed: only the sleeve preload and sliding friction remain (near 0 N), so the stack bends freely.\n7. For 300 N: need 300/24 = 12.5, so 13 interfaces, i.e. 14 layers.\n8. Practical limits: the vacuum cannot exceed 101 kPa, real friction coefficients and uneven loading reduce capacity, and more layers add thickness and bending stiffness in the soft state.","answers":["≈ 120 N at 80 kPa vacuum (about 0 N unjammed)","≈ 14 layers for 300 N (13 interfaces)"]},{"id":"8.4","title":"Designing a soft arm for keyhole surgery","q":"Design a soft continuum manipulator that passes through a 12 mm laparoscopic trocar, reaches organs in the abdomen, and can push back on tissue with about 1 N at its tip. Choose the body material and fabrication, actuation, stiffness-tuning method, sensing and control. Justify each choice and state the main risks and trade-offs.","figs":["figures/sr/fig8-28.webp"],"figDesc":"Cutaway rendering of a soft cylindrical module with internal chambers, and photographs of prototype segments that elongate and bend in several directions.","sol":"1. Requirements: diameter under 12 mm, dexterity (bending in any direction, elongation), tip force about 1 N, tissue-safe materials, sterilizable.\n2. Body: modular silicone elastomer segments, molded with internal chambers and a central working channel; fibers or braid limit radial expansion.\n3. Actuation: pneumatic or hydraulic chambers (three per segment) for omnidirectional bending and elongation; high strain and force but needs external pressure supply, so hydraulics with saline are an option to limit compressibility. Tendons are an alternative but risk concentrated loads.\n4. Stiffness tuning: vacuum granular or layer jamming gives a large, fast, controllable stiffness increase without volume change, so the arm is soft for insertion and stiff to push; alternatives (thermoplastic, MR or ER) are slower or need large fields.\n5. Sensing: embedded fiber-optic or flexible curvature sensors for shape (proprioception) and force/contact sensing at the tip; closed-loop pressure control with a kinematic model.\n6. Risks: hysteresis and compliance limit accuracy, vacuum lines add stiffness, and sterility and biocompatibility need qualification.","answers":["Modular silicone body, pneumatic/hydraulic chambers for omnidirectional bending, jamming for variable stiffness","Embedded fiber-optic or flexible sensors plus pressure feedback","Trade-offs: soft insertion versus 1 N tip force, lines and pumps, accuracy"]},{"id":"8.5","title":"Magnetically steered biopsy capsule design","q":"Design a soft capsule robot for fine-needle biopsy in the stomach, driven by an external electromagnet array and a tether. The capsule contains a permanent magnet with dipole moment 0.5 A·m², is 5 g, and sits in a 20 mT field. First compute the maximum magnetic torque and compare it with the gravitational torque of the capsule weight acting through a 10 mm lever arm. Then choose the compliant mechanism, motion modes, sensing and anti-slip features, and explain how the needle is deployed.","figs":["figures/sr/fig8-37.webp"],"figDesc":"Left: illustration of a capsule in the stomach with biopsy, retraction and tumor labels. Right: lab setup with a workspace surrounded by an electromagnet array; sketch of the capsule with tether, camera, magnet and anti-slip pads.","sol":"1. Maximum torque τ = mB = 0.5 × 0.020 = 1.0×10⁻² N·m = 10 mN·m.\n2. Gravitational torque = m g d = 0.005 × 9.81 × 0.010 = 4.9×10⁻⁴ N·m = 0.49 mN·m, so the magnetic torque is about 20 times larger and can orient and roll the capsule.\n3. Mechanism: a compliant Sarrus linkage with flexure hinges so that axial compression pushes a needle out and spring-back retracts it; the stiff-soft body protects the tissue.\n4. Motion modes: field-orientation and rolling locomotion, anchoring toward a tumor by a lateral force, and a collapsing mode where a large field gradient compresses the linkage and drives the needle; angular stiffness rises from about 1 to 49 mN·m/rad between modes.\n5. Sensing: onboard camera, tether for power and biopsy retrieval, external magnetic or visual tracking of pose.\n6. Anti-slip pads on the body for traction on the wet mucosa.\n7. Risks: field gradient and torque fall quickly with distance from the magnet array, field coverage limits the workspace, and needle force is limited by the magnetic force available.","answers":["Max magnetic torque ≈ 10 mN·m, about 20 times the gravity torque of ≈ 0.49 mN·m","Compliant Sarrus linkage with flexure hinges, needle driven by field-induced collapse, anti-slip pads, camera and tether","Motion modes: orientation or rolling, tumor anchoring, collapse for needle penetration"]},{"id":"9.1","title":"Tip deflection of a silicone cantilever limb","q":"A soft limb is modeled as a clamped-free Euler-Bernoulli beam of rectangular cross-section: width b = 10 mm, thickness h = 2 mm, length L = 50 mm, Young\u0027s modulus E = 2 MPa. A transverse tip force V = 2 mN is applied. (a) Compute the flexural rigidity D = EI and the linear-theory tip deflection. (b) Compute the dimensionless load VL²/D and state whether the small-deflection assumption is acceptable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Euler-Bernoulli cantilever, M = V(L - x), curvature M/D.\n2. Second moment: I = bh³/12 = (0.010)(0.002)³/12 = 6.67e-12 m⁴.\n3. D = EI = 2e6 × 6.67e-12 = 1.33e-5 N·m².\n4. Integrating v\u0027\u0027 = V(L - x)/D with v(0) = v\u0027(0) = 0 gives tip deflection δ = VL³/(3D).\n5. δ = (0.002)(0.05)³/(3 × 1.33e-5) = 2.5e-7/4.0e-5 = 6.25 mm (12.5 percent of L).\n6. VL²/D = (0.002)(0.0025)/1.33e-5 = 0.375. The tip slope is about 0.19 rad so cosθ ≈ 0.98 and the Elastica differs from the linear solution by only a few percent; the linear result is acceptable. Beyond VL²/D of order 1 the Elastica should be used.","answers":["D ≈ 1.33e-5 N·m²","Tip deflection ≈ 6.25 mm (12.5% of L)","VL²/D ≈ 0.375, small-deflection theory acceptable (error of a few percent)"]},{"id":"9.2","title":"One implicit time step of a damped soft element","q":"A single-degree-of-freedom soft element has mass m = 0.02 kg, internal force F(q, v) = k q + c v with k = 500 N/m and c = 0.2 N·s/m. It is at rest at q = 0 and a constant external force p = 1 N is applied. A simulation uses implicit Euler with time step h = 5 ms and a Taylor-linearized internal force, with no constraints. (a) Find the system coefficient A = m + h ∂F/∂v + h² ∂F/∂q. (b) Find the velocity and position after one step.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: linearized implicit step A dv = b with b = -h² (∂F/∂q) v_n - h (f_n - p_{n+1}).\n2. Derivatives: ∂F/∂v = c = 0.2, ∂F/∂q = k = 500.\n3. A = 0.02 + (0.005)(0.2) + (0.005)²(500) = 0.02 + 0.001 + 0.0125 = 0.0335 kg.\n4. At rest, v_n = 0 and f_n = 0, so b = h p = 0.005 N·s.\n5. dv = b/A = 0.005/0.0335 = 0.149 m/s, so v_{n+1} = 0.149 m/s.\n6. q_{n+1} = q_n + h v_{n+1} = 0.005 × 0.149 = 7.5e-4 m.\n7. Check: m(v₁ - 0) = h(p - k q₁ - c v₁) with q₁ = h v₁ gives the same result.","answers":["A = 0.0335 kg","v after one step ≈ 0.149 m/s","q after one step ≈ 0.75 mm"]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Step length of a three-segment crawler","q":"A planar crawler of total length 100 mm is modeled as three inextensible rod segments in contact with the ground: S1 (30 mm), S2 (40 mm) and S3 (30 mm). During the contraction phase S1 and S3 each lift into an arch described by y = h sin(πs/L_i) with peak height h = 3 mm, while S2 stays flat on the ground. Using the small-slope span formula Λ = Σ ∫ {1 - ½ (y\u0027)²} ds, find (a) the horizontal span Λ in the arched state and (b) the maximum displacement per cycle if the rear end always stays anchored while the body is extending and the front end stays anchored while it is contracting.","figs":["figures/sr/fig9-23.webp"],"figDesc":"Diagram of a three-segment robot S1, S2, S3 resting on a hatched ground line, drawn as elastic rods (green, blue, orange) of arclength s with vertical deflection y(s); formulas for a loaded beam are written beneath.","sol":"1. Approach: vertical arching of an inextensible rod shortens its horizontal projection by ½∫(y\u0027)² ds.\n2. For y = h sin(πs/L), y\u0027 = (hπ/L) cos(πs/L), so ∫₀ᴸ (y\u0027)² ds = (hπ/L)² (L/2) = h²π²/(2L).\n3. For one arch: h²π²/(2L) = (9)(9.870)/(60) = 1.480 mm, so the span loss is ½ × 1.480 = 0.740 mm.\n4. Two arches (S1 and S3): total loss 1.48 mm.\n5. Λ = 100 - 1.48 = 98.52 mm.\n6. Extension and contraction each change the end separation by 1.48 mm. With anchoring roles alternating as stated, the body advances by the full change each cycle: 1.48 mm, about 1.5 percent of body length.","answers":["Λ ≈ 98.5 mm in the arched state (loss 1.48 mm)","Maximum displacement per cycle ≈ 1.5 mm (about 1.5% of body length)"]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Choose a simulation strategy for a soft quadruped","q":"Your team is designing a pneumatic soft quadruped (silicone limbs, hyperelastic, contact with ground and friction). Early on you need to sweep dozens of limb geometries and gait timings; later you want a real-time model for model-based control. Propose a simulation strategy: which models you would use in each phase (full FEA, reduced-order FEA, beam or rod models), what you would validate and how, and what the main risks are.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Early design sweep: discretize each limb as a reduced-dimension element (Euler-Bernoulli or Elastica beam with tunable D and natural curvature, Coulomb friction at tip and side contacts) so cheap solves expose scaling laws and let you run many gaits.\n2. Use a geometrically exact rod model (Discrete Elastic Rods) when deflections are large; its banded Jacobian keeps each Newton solve cheap and contact and friction can be included.\n3. Use full 3D FEA sparingly, for a few finalists, to capture local stress, chamber ballooning and mesh-converged behavior.\n4. For control, build a model-order-reduced FEM (modes from FEA, projected implicit Euler step) or a calibrated rod model that runs in real time.\n5. Validation: material tests (hyperelastic coefficients), motion capture of tip pose versus pressure, ground displacement versus gait on several surfaces; compare trends, not just one number.\n6. Risks: friction and material uncertainty (garbage in, garbage out), non-convergence, reduced-model accuracy outside the sampled modes.","answers":["Cheap reduced-dimension beam/rod (DER) models for sweeps, FEA only for finalists, reduced-order FEM or calibrated rod model for control","Validate against material tests, motion capture and displacement on several surfaces","Main risks: friction and material uncertainty, convergence, reduced-model range"]},{"id":"9.5","title":"Why rods beat linear beams for soft limbs","q":"A colleague proposes modeling a soft pneumatic finger that curls to nearly 180 degrees using Euler-Bernoulli linear beam theory, because it is closed-form. Explain what is wrong with this choice, which theory or numerical method you would use instead, what the governing equations and numerical solver look like, and why that choice remains computationally cheap.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Linear beam theory assumes dv/ds = θ (small slope) and computes the moment in the reference configuration, so for large rotation it misestimates moment arms and deflections; the error grows with VL²/D (visible beyond about 0.5 to 1).\n2. Use Elastica (dv/ds = sinθ, moments in the current configuration, d²θ/ds² = -(V/D) cosθ) for planar quasi-static loading, or Discrete Elastic Rods for dynamics, contact, twist and stretch.\n3. DER discretizes the rod into nodes with positions and orientation, with bending, twisting and stretching energies; forces are F = -∇E; an implicit step is solved with Newton, Δv ← Δv - J⁻¹F.\n4. Cheapness: each node\u0027s force depends only on neighbors, so J is banded and each solve scales linearly with node count; N equations in N DOFs, geometrically exact with no small-deformation assumption.\n5. Still validate natural curvature and stiffness versus pressure experimentally.","answers":["Linear theory fails at large rotation (cosθ is not about 1, moments computed in the wrong configuration)","Use Elastica or Discrete Elastic Rods (geometrically exact, energy-based, Newton solve)","Banded Jacobian gives cheap, near-linear-cost solves"]},{"id":"10.1","title":"Peel force of a wide adhesive film","q":"A strip of adhesive tape of width w = 25 mm is peeled from a rigid substrate. The interfacial adhesion energy is γ = 0.2 J/m². Use the Kendall peel model. (a) For a very stiff backing, find the steady peel force at peel angles of 90° and 30°. (b) For a soft film of thickness h = 0.1 mm and Young\u0027s modulus E = 2 MPa peeled at an angle approaching 0°, find the peel force.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Kendall equation (1/2Eh)(F/w)² + (F/w)(1 - cosθ) - γ = 0.\n2. Stiff backing (E → ∞): the quadratic term vanishes, F = wγ/(1 - cosθ).\n3. θ = 90°: F = 0.025 × 0.2 = 5.0e-3 N = 5 mN.\n4. θ = 30°: 1 - cos30° = 0.134, F = 5.0 mN/0.134 = 37.3 mN.\n5. θ → 0: the (1 - cosθ) term vanishes, (F/w)² = 2Ehγ.\n6. F/w = √(2 × 2e6 × 1e-4 × 0.2) = √80 = 8.94 N/m.\n7. F = 8.94 × 0.025 = 0.224 N.\n8. Note the force does not depend on peeled length.","answers":["90°: ≈ 5 mN","30°: ≈ 37 mN","θ → 0 for the soft film: ≈ 0.22 N, independent of peel length"]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Gain from splitting a flat-punch contact","q":"A silicone flat punch of radius R = 5 mm (modulus E = 2 MPa, Poisson ratio 0.5) is pulled from a rigid substrate with interfacial adhesion energy γ = 0.1 J/m². Use the flat-punch pull-off relation F ≈ (8πE*R³γ)^½ and the effective modulus for a compliant body against a rigid one. (a) Find the single-punch pull-off force. (b) The same total contact area is divided into 100 identical flat punches. Find the new total pull-off force and the improvement factor.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: rigid counter-surface so 1/E* = (1 - ν²)/E.\n2. E* = 2e6/(1 - 0.25) = 2.67 MPa.\n3. Single punch: R³ = 1.25e-7 m³. 8π E* R³ γ = 25.13 × 2.67e6 × 1.25e-7 × 0.1 = 0.838 N². F = √0.838 = 0.915 N.\n4. Equal total area with n = 100: each punch has radius R/√n = 0.5 mm.\n5. F_each = √(8π E* (5e-4)³ γ) = 0.915 × (0.1)^{1.5} = 0.0289 N.\n6. Total = 100 × 0.0289 = 2.89 N.\n7. Improvement factor = n^{1/4} = 100^{0.25} = 3.16.","answers":["Single punch: ≈ 0.92 N","100 punches of equal total area: ≈ 2.9 N total","Improvement factor n^(1/4) ≈ 3.2"]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Will a curled film stay bonded to a substrate","q":"A thin soft film is bonded to a flat rigid substrate with interfacial adhesion energy γ = 0.2 J/m². Its flexural rigidity per unit width is D = 1.56e-5 N·m (E = 1.5 MPa, thickness 0.5 mm, treat as a beam). The film has a stress-free natural curvature κ₀ that tries to curl it up from the substrate. Using the moment criterion M₀ = Dκ₀ ≤ M_a = (2Dγ)^½, find (a) the adhesion moment per unit width M_a, (b) the largest natural curvature and smallest natural radius that remain bonded, and (c) whether films with natural radius 8 mm and 5 mm remain bonded.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the film delaminates when the internal moment exceeds M_a.\n2. M_a = √(2Dγ) = √(2 × 1.56e-5 × 0.2) = √(6.24e-6) = 2.5e-3 N (moment per unit width).\n3. Condition Dκ₀ ≤ M_a gives κ₀ ≤ √(2γ/D) = √(0.4/1.56e-5) = √25640 = 160 m⁻¹.\n4. Minimum radius = 1/160 = 6.25 mm.\n5. R = 8 mm: κ₀ = 125 m⁻¹ \u003c 160, so it stays bonded.\n6. R = 5 mm: κ₀ = 200 m⁻¹ \u003e 160, so M₀ = 3.1e-3 N exceeds M_a and the film peels or curls.","answers":["M_a ≈ 2.5e-3 N per unit width","κ₀ max ≈ 160 m⁻¹ (radius ≥ 6.25 mm)","8 mm radius stays bonded; 5 mm radius delaminates"]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Gripper for smooth, curved and crinkled objects","q":"Design a soft gripper that must pick up a glass lightbulb, a ceramic cup, a tomato and a crinkled snack bag from a bin and release them quickly, with a maximum payload of about 3 N per pad. Propose the adhesion strategy (adhesive type, backing, how load is distributed), the actuation and release method, and the main failure modes. Justify using contact-mechanics scaling.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Adhesive: dry microfiber (gecko-type) pads exploit contact splitting, so pull-off scales with fiber radius R (sphere/JKR, F = 1.5πRγ per contact) or R^1.5 for flat tips, so many small contacts give more total force than one large patch.\n2. Backing: a soft inflatable or pressure-controlled membrane gives macroscopic conformity to curved objects and shares load uniformly so fibers do not peel one by one from the edge (GeckoGripper II, pull-off of a few N at about -50 kPa on flat substrates).\n3. Actuation: use pressure difference to engage and equalize load; releasing the pressure drops adhesion; peeling at an angle (small load per fiber) aids release.\n4. Alternatives or supplements: electroadhesion for fast on/off (under 50 ms) on non-conductive surfaces, or a pneumatic finger for tomatoes.\n5. Failure modes: dust and wet surfaces reducing γ, fiber collapse or matting, edge peeling when load is not shared, cavity leakage, insufficient conformity on crinkled film (needs compliant backing).","answers":["Microfiber (gecko-type) pads exploit contact splitting; compliant pressure-controlled membrane gives conformity and uniform load sharing","Pressure or electric field switches adhesion on and off for quick release","Risks: contamination, edge peeling, leakage, limited payload on very rough or wet surfaces"]},{"id":"10.5","title":"Electroadhesive pad for wall and object handling","q":"An electroadhesive pad of 8 cm × 8 cm is proposed for hanging a payload against a vertical wall. Lateral holding force per unit area was measured at 0.41 N/cm² on glass and 0.21 N/cm² on drywall. (a) With a safety factor of 2, find the maximum payload mass the pad can hold in shear on each surface (g = 9.81 m/s²). (b) Explain how electroadhesion works on conductive and non-conductive substrates, what limits its use, and when you would prefer a gecko-type or vacuum alternative.","figs":["figures/sr/fig10-48.webp"],"figDesc":"Photos of an electroadhesive pad (square frame with interdigitated electrode lines) mounted on wood, glass and steel, and a table listing for each material the measured lateral force per unit area (N/cm²), measured friction coefficient and estimated normal pressure (N/cm²).","sol":"1. Pad area A = 8 × 8 = 64 cm².\n2. Glass: ultimate shear force = 0.41 × 64 = 26.2 N; with safety factor 2, allowable 13.1 N; mass = 13.1/9.81 = 1.34 kg.\n3. Drywall: 0.21 × 64 = 13.4 N; allowable 6.7 N; mass = 0.68 kg.\n4. Mechanism: compliant interdigitated electrodes with a compliant clamp conform to roughness; on conductive substrates clamping arises from Lorentz-type forces, on non-conductive ones from polarization forces. Its attraction is electrostatic but powered, so it switches in under 50 ms.\n5. Limits: needs a high voltage supply, holding drops with humidity, rough surfaces, and force depends on the substrate material; consumes power while holding (low, since little current).\n6. Alternatives: gecko-type dry adhesive when no power is wanted and surfaces are smooth and clean; vacuum for porous-free large flat objects needing high force.","answers":["Glass: ≈ 1.3 kg; drywall: ≈ 0.68 kg (with safety factor 2)","Conductive: Lorentz-type clamping; non-conductive: polarization forces; both switch in under 50 ms","Limits: high voltage, substrate and humidity dependence; gecko or vacuum for passive or high-force cases"]},{"id":"11.1","title":"Corner frequency of a dry skin electrode","q":"A surface EMG electrode on skin is modelled as a series resistance Rs = 1.5 kΩ in series with a parallel combination of Rd = 120 kΩ and Cd = 20 nF. Find (a) the frequency at which the interface RC has its corner, (b) the magnitude of the total impedance at that frequency, and (c) the magnitude of the impedance at 1 kHz.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Model: Z = Rs + Rd/(1 + j2πf Cd Rd).\n2. Corner frequency fc = 1/(2π Rd Cd) = 1/(2π × 1.2e5 × 2e-8) = 1/(0.01508) = 66.3 Hz.\n3. At fc the term 2πf Cd Rd = 1, so Rd/(1+j) = Rd(1−j)/2 = 60 − j60 kΩ. Z = 61.5 − j60 kΩ, |Z| = sqrt(61.5² + 60²) = 85.9 kΩ.\n4. At 1 kHz: 2πf Cd Rd = 2π × 1000 × 2.4e-3 = 15.08; 1 + 15.08² = 228.4. Rd/(1+j15.08) = 120 kΩ (1 − j15.08)/228.4 = 525 − j7923 Ω.\n5. Z = 2025 − j7923 Ω, |Z| = sqrt(2025² + 7923²) = 8.18 kΩ.\n6. Interpretation: impedance falls from about Rs+Rd = 121.5 kΩ at DC toward Rs = 1.5 kΩ at high frequency; a better (conformal) electrode lowers Rd and raises Cd.","answers":["fc ≈ 66 Hz","|Z(fc)| ≈ 85.9 kΩ","|Z(1 kHz)| ≈ 8.2 kΩ"]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Force capacity of a pneumatic tactile cell","q":"A circular pneumatic tactile cell in a haptic display has a flexible membrane of effective diameter 2.0 in. (50.8 mm) filled with granular particles. It is inflated to a gauge pressure of 1.2 psi (1 psi = 6895 Pa). (a) What force on a flat plate pressed against the dome balances the pressure force at this pressure? (b) What is it at 0.6 psi? (c) Explain what vacuum applied to the particle chamber changes and why it matters for a haptic display.","figs":["figures/sr/fig11-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Single cell: a vacuum chamber with particles under a membrane, an air-pressure line with electronic regulator and air cylinders; and an array of cells of different sizes under a silicone skin that can be inflated individually.","sol":"1. Approach: quasi-static pressure balance, F = P × A on the membrane footprint.\n2. Area A = π d²/4 = π (0.0508)²/4 = 2.027e-3 m².\n3. P1 = 1.2 psi = 1.2 × 6895 = 8274 Pa. F1 = 8274 × 2.027e-3 = 16.8 N.\n4. P2 = 0.6 psi = 4137 Pa, so F2 = 8.4 N (force is linear in pressure).\n5. Vacuum draws the membrane tight on the particles, which jam through frictional contact: the cell becomes much stiffer at fixed shape. Inflation then sets geometry (shape) and vacuum sets impedance (stiffness), so shape and stiffness are controlled independently.\n6. This lets one surface render both a bump and how hard it feels; the cost is pumps, valves and response time of seconds.","answers":["F ≈ 16.8 N at 1.2 psi","F ≈ 8.4 N at 0.6 psi","Vacuum jams particles, raising stiffness independent of inflation-set shape"]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Battery life of a pneumatic rehabilitation glove","q":"A soft rehabilitation glove has five fingers, each actuator needing 4.0 mL of fluid at a gauge pressure of 150 kPa per flexion. A therapy session cycles the glove at 0.5 Hz (one full flexion-extension every 2 s); fluid is vented on extension, so only pressurisation costs energy. Overall pump-and-valve efficiency is 20 percent. The battery is 11.1 V, 2000 mAh. Estimate (a) hydraulic energy per cycle, (b) average electrical power, (c) battery run time.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: energy per cycle = P ΔV per actuator, summed, then divided by efficiency.\n2. Per actuator: 150e3 Pa × 4e-6 m³ = 0.60 J. Five actuators: 3.0 J per cycle.\n3. Hydraulic power at 0.5 Hz: 3.0 × 0.5 = 1.5 W.\n4. Electrical power = 1.5/0.20 = 7.5 W.\n5. Battery energy = 11.1 V × 2.0 Ah = 22.2 Wh = 79.9 kJ.\n6. Run time = 79 920 J / 7.5 W = 10 660 s = 2.96 h, about 3 hours of continuous cycling.\n7. Comment: the pump and valves dominate size and mass, which is why portability and wearability are central questions for pneumatic gloves.","answers":["3.0 J per cycle","7.5 W electrical","about 3.0 h of run time"]},{"id":"11.4","title":"Design a soft glove for thumb rehabilitation","q":"A clinic wants a wearable soft glove for home rehabilitation of thumb opposition after stroke, to be worn for 1 hour sessions and used with minimal supervision. Choose the actuation, materials, transmission to the finger and the sensing/control strategy, say how you would evaluate it clinically, and justify the trade-offs.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: safe near a weak, spastic hand, light, conformal, adjustable across users, quiet, enough thumb flexion, opposition and abduction, 1 h battery life.\n2. Actuation: fibre-reinforced elastomer bending/extending actuators (pneumatic or hydraulic) along each finger and an extra actuator for thumb abduction; soft, force-limited by pressure, no joint-axis alignment. Alternative: tendon/SMA (smaller, but slow and hot).\n3. Materials: silicone body with an inextensible layer and helical fibres so pressure yields bending; textile glove for donning and fixation.\n4. Sensing and control: bend sensors or pressure sensors in the glove, EMG of the intact or partially working muscles for intent (assist-as-needed), pressure regulator with safety relief valve.\n5. Evaluation: Kapandji index for thumb opposition, box-and-block test, range of motion and grip force, user comfort and compliance.\n6. Trade-offs: pump and valve bulk and noise versus portability; fluid choice (air compliant but compressible versus water stiffer and heavier).","answers":["Fibre-reinforced soft pneumatic/hydraulic actuators with force limiting by pressure, thumb-specific actuator, textile glove","EMG or bend sensing for assist-as-needed control with safety relief","Evaluate with Kapandji index and box-and-block test; trade portability against pump size and noise"]},{"id":"11.5","title":"Choose an EMG electrode for prosthesis control","q":"You must select a skin electrode for a wearable controller that reads forearm EMG to drive a prosthetic hand all day. Candidates are Ag/AgCl hydrogel, a dry stainless-steel contact, and a thin conformal tattoo-type electrode with stretchable liquid-metal traces. Compare them using a skin-electrode impedance model and recommend one, including signal quality, comfort and manufacturing considerations.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Model: Z = Rs + Rd/(1 + j2πf Cd Rd); the EMG band is roughly tens to hundreds of Hz, so we want low Rd and large Cd (large real contact area, Cd = εA/d).\n2. Dry stainless steel: rigid, touches only skin asperities, so high Rd and low Cd, high impedance in band, noisy and motion-artefact prone, but durable and cheap.\n3. Ag/AgCl hydrogel: clinical standard, low impedance and low noise, but gel dries out in hours, irritates skin and is not stretchable.\n4. Conformal tattoo/hydrogel-PDMS electrode: thin and soft, follows skin, large real contact area, lowest Rd and highest Cd and the best signal-to-noise ratio in comparisons, stays in place with skin motion.\n5. Recommendation: conformal stretchable electrode (hydrogel-PDMS or tattoo) for all-day wear, with Ag/AgCl as a reference; use EGaIn or printed Ag-In-Ga traces to a small flexible board.\n6. Considerations: wear time, skin irritation, cost and fabrication (printing or laser patterning), connector reliability.","answers":["Impedance model: want low Rd, high Cd in the EMG band; conformal contact helps","Dry rigid electrodes are noisy; Ag/AgCl dries out; conformal tattoo/hydrogel-PDMS gives best signal and comfort","Recommend a conformal stretchable electrode with stretchable traces for all-day use"]},{"id":"12.1","title":"Jump of a combustion-powered soft robot","q":"A 0.40 kg untethered soft robot with a butane combustion actuator jumps to a peak height of 0.60 m above the ground. Take g = 9.81 m/s², neglect drag, and assume the take-off speed is reached over an actuator stroke of 5.0 cm. Find (a) the take-off speed, (b) the average acceleration during the stroke, in units of g, ignoring gravity during the stroke, and (c) the butane mass required if 5 percent of its chemical energy becomes jump kinetic energy (butane energy density 49 MJ/kg).","figs":["figures/sr/fig12-22.webp"],"figDesc":"3D-printed soft robot with a central core module, pneumatic legs, protective shield and an explosive (combustion) actuator, plus a system schematic with high-voltage source, circuit board, battery, oxygen and butane supplies, mini air compressor, pressure regulator and six valves.","sol":"1. Approach: energy balance on a ballistic jump.\n2. Take-off speed from ½mv² = mgh: v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(2 × 9.81 × 0.60) = sqrt(11.77) = 3.43 m/s.\n3. Constant acceleration over stroke s: a = v²/(2s) = 11.77/(2 × 0.05) = 117.7 m/s² = 12.0 g.\n4. Kinetic energy = mgh = 0.40 × 9.81 × 0.60 = 2.35 J.\n5. Chemical energy needed = 2.35/0.05 = 47.1 J.\n6. Butane mass = 47.1 J / 49e6 J/kg = 9.6e-7 kg = 0.96 mg.\n7. Insight: very little fuel is needed; the difficulty is releasing the energy in milliseconds and surviving the landing, hence the functionally graded body.","answers":["v ≈ 3.43 m/s","a ≈ 118 m/s² ≈ 12 g","Butane ≈ 0.96 mg (about 1 mg)"]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Speed and cycle time of an SMA crawler","q":"An untethered SMA crawling robot is 4.0 cm long. Each actuation cycle moves it 3.0 cm, with 2.0 s of heating and 10.0 s of cooling in still air. (a) Find its speed in cm/s and in body lengths per second. (b) What cycle time would give 1 body length per second? (c) For an SMA wire of diameter 0.15 mm (density 6450 kg/m³, specific heat 500 J/(kg·K)), estimate the lumped cooling time constant τ = ρ c d/(4h) for h = 100 W/(m²·K) in air and h = 1000 W/(m²·K) in water.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: kinematics, then lumped-capacitance cooling.\n2. Cycle time = 2 + 10 = 12 s, so v = 3.0 cm / 12 s = 0.25 cm/s.\n3. Relative speed = 0.25/4.0 = 0.0625 body lengths per second.\n4. For 1 blps, v = 4.0 cm/s; with 3.0 cm per cycle, cycle time = 3.0/4.0 = 0.75 s, 16 times faster than now.\n5. Cooling: a wire of length L has volume π d² L/4 and surface π d L, so the ratio is d/4. τ = ρ c d/(4h).\n6. ρ c d = 6450 × 500 × 1.5e-4 = 483.8 J/(m²·K).\n7. Air: τ = 483.8/(4 × 100) = 1.21 s. Water: τ = 483.8/(4 × 1000) = 0.12 s.\n8. The assumed 10 s cooling is about 8τ in air, so the lumped model suggests the real cycle is conservative; but cooling still dominates the cycle, and water or thinner wires would shorten it.","answers":["v = 0.25 cm/s = 0.0625 blps","Cycle time 0.75 s for 1 blps","τ ≈ 1.2 s in air, ≈ 0.12 s in water"]},{"id":"12.3","title":"Speed of a peristaltic worm robot","q":"An earthworm-inspired robot has 8 identical segments. Each segment is 25 mm long when elongated and 18 mm when contracted. A single contraction wave travels from tail to head at one wave per 2.0 s (0.5 Hz). Assume no slip, so that each point on the body advances by the change in length of one segment per wave. Find (a) the stride per wave, (b) the robot speed in mm/s, (c) the speed in body lengths per second, and (d) the wave speed along the body if the wave takes 0.25 s to move one segment.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: kinematics of peristalsis with anchoring.\n2. Stride per wave = 25 − 18 = 7 mm.\n3. Speed = stride × frequency = 7 mm × 0.5 Hz = 3.5 mm/s.\n4. Body length (elongated) = 8 × 25 = 200 mm, so relative speed = 3.5/200 = 0.0175 body lengths per second.\n5. Wave speed along the body: one segment (about 25 mm) per 0.25 s = 100 mm/s, which is much faster than the robot speed (about 29 times), as expected for peristalsis.\n6. Comment: to increase speed use larger contraction ratio, more frequent waves or overlapping waves; the limit is actuator bandwidth and friction anchoring.","answers":["Stride 7 mm per wave","v = 3.5 mm/s ≈ 0.0175 body lengths/s","Wave speed ≈ 100 mm/s"]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Design an untethered underwater soft robot","q":"Design an untethered soft robot that must swim and crawl in a shallow pool for 30 minutes to inspect the bottom, carrying a small camera. Choose the locomotion mode, actuator, power source, body materials, sensing and control, and justify your choices against the alternatives (fin-based fish, brittle-star-like limbs, pneumatic crawler).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: 30 minute endurance, camera payload, waterproofing, safe handling, steering ability, moderate speed.\n2. Locomotion: for swimming, body-caudal-fin undulation is efficient and quiet; for bottom contact and steering, radial multi-limb stepping (brittle star) lets it move in any direction without turning. Choose according to mission: fish for open water, brittle-star limbs for crawling and manoeuvring.\n3. Actuation: SMA coils in silicone arms (cooled by water, electrical only, simple) or a fluidic elastomer tail with onboard pump; SMA is slow, pneumatic needs pump and valves.\n4. Power: LiPo battery and MOSFET drivers; compare energy: SMA draws high current, so check the 30 minute budget.\n5. Materials: silicone body, foam for buoyancy, sealed electronics island, soft skin around the rigid parts.\n6. Sensing/control: camera, IMU or tracking by external overhead camera, Bluetooth link, closed-loop gait selection.\n7. Trade-offs: speed versus softness, autonomy versus tether, cooling versus efficiency.","answers":["Choose fin undulation for open water or SMA brittle-star limbs for manoeuvring on the bottom","Battery-powered SMA (water-cooled) or fluidic tail with pump; buoyant sealed electronics","Justify with endurance, speed, steering and robustness trade-offs; closed-loop sensing and wireless control"]},{"id":"12.5","title":"Pick a locomotion strategy for rubble search","q":"A rescue team wants a palm-sized soft robot that can enter collapsed-building rubble, squeeze through gaps, survive being stepped on and carry gas sensors for several hours. Compare tethered and untethered operation and recommend a locomotion mode, actuation and power strategy, with the main failure risks.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Needs: squeeze through gaps, impact survival, long endurance, moderate speed, confined spaces.\n2. Tether option: pneumatic or electrical tether gives unlimited energy and simple actuators, but snags in rubble and limits range; good for short probes.\n3. Untethered option: carries power; endurance limited by battery mass, especially for pneumatics (pump) and SMA (current).\n4. Locomotion: peristaltic or soft crawling shapes (meshworm style) fit through gaps by compressing and are robust; legged pneumatic walkers survive loads but need pumps; jumping crosses obstacles but is hard to control and lands uncontrolled.\n5. Recommendation: hybrid, a peristaltic or crawling body with low-power actuators (e.g. dielectric elastomer or low-duty SMA), a thin tether for power and data in the first version, moving to onboard battery with duty cycling.\n6. Risks: actuator slowness, overheating, sealing, snagging, loss of communication, battery mass.","answers":["Tether gives power but snags; untethered limited by battery and slow actuators","Peristaltic/crawling body with soft compliant skin suits gaps and impacts","Hybrid or duty-cycled low-power design; main risks are snagging, slow actuation, power and communication"]}];
